WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.864
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.272 --> 00:00:08.480
CARTER MORGAN: Hi, and
welcome back to VM End-to-End,

00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:11.480
a show where we have
a VM skeptic, myself,

00:00:11.480 --> 00:00:13.868
and a VM enthusiast,
or VM enthusiasts,

00:00:13.868 --> 00:00:15.410
come on to the show
to talk about why

00:00:15.410 --> 00:00:17.720
VMs are cool,
interesting, and useful

00:00:17.720 --> 00:00:19.280
for a cloud-native future.

00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:23.533
Last time, we talked about
reasoning about reliability.

00:00:23.533 --> 00:00:24.950
But I feel like I
still don't know

00:00:24.950 --> 00:00:27.210
enough to actually do anything
with it in the real world.

00:00:27.210 --> 00:00:28.752
So we've brought
Steve and Brian back

00:00:28.752 --> 00:00:30.560
on today to help
hammer that out.

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:31.200
Hi, Steve.

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:31.700
Hi, Brian.

00:00:31.700 --> 00:00:32.600
Thanks for being here.

00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:33.683
STEVE MCGHEE: Hey, Carter.

00:00:33.683 --> 00:00:35.577
Nice to be here.

00:00:35.577 --> 00:00:36.910
CARTER MORGAN: Glad to have you.

00:00:36.910 --> 00:00:39.800
Brian, I got to start
with you, buddy.

00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:41.330
After last week's
episode, do you

00:00:41.330 --> 00:00:45.410
think I know enough to actually
go and like take a small SaaS

00:00:45.410 --> 00:00:48.470
offering, and go from three nine
survivability to four nines,

00:00:48.470 --> 00:00:51.340
to maybe five nines?

00:00:51.340 --> 00:00:53.890
BRIAN DORSEY: So I think we've
got a lot of the components.

00:00:53.890 --> 00:00:58.000
We talked about some of the
patterns that are common.

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:01.390
And we can dig into that
a little more as well.

00:01:01.390 --> 00:01:04.690
But the question is kind
of like, when and how?

00:01:04.690 --> 00:01:07.360
So what if we got a
little more specific here?

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:11.830
Like, what kind of app are
you imagining building?

00:01:11.830 --> 00:01:12.580
CARTER MORGAN: OK.

00:01:12.580 --> 00:01:15.400
Let's go with a
payment processing app.

00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:16.828
So it's a SaaS application.

00:01:16.828 --> 00:01:18.370
And it's going to
exist in the cloud.

00:01:18.370 --> 00:01:20.500
People do payments.

00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:23.170
Right now, that's mostly
what it's going to focus on.

00:01:23.170 --> 00:01:26.620
I guess it's-- to be honest, I
don't even know how to reason

00:01:26.620 --> 00:01:28.935
about how reliable
that is by itself.

00:01:28.935 --> 00:01:30.310
So I guess we
should start there.

00:01:30.310 --> 00:01:31.377
Steve?

00:01:31.377 --> 00:01:33.460
STEVE MCGHEE: Yeah, this
is a good place to start.

00:01:33.460 --> 00:01:35.290
Like, I think using--

00:01:35.290 --> 00:01:39.010
in SRE, we call this a
non-abstract systems design

00:01:39.010 --> 00:01:41.060
problem, which is the
best way to do it.

00:01:41.060 --> 00:01:44.440
And I think, if you
think about this,

00:01:44.440 --> 00:01:46.390
you're building a
system on your laptop,

00:01:46.390 --> 00:01:48.640
probably, or on your desktop.

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:50.685
You're getting it
functionally correct first.

00:01:50.685 --> 00:01:52.060
And then you have
to think about,

00:01:52.060 --> 00:01:54.670
how much do I want to
invest in into it in order

00:01:54.670 --> 00:01:58.300
to scale it up, to have it do
something for someone else?

00:01:58.300 --> 00:02:00.640
Because having it just
run tests on your laptop

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:02.740
doesn't really make
you a lot of money,

00:02:02.740 --> 00:02:04.330
doesn't do much
good for society.

00:02:04.330 --> 00:02:05.530
You want to get it
out there, right?

00:02:05.530 --> 00:02:07.070
You want to put it
into production.

00:02:07.070 --> 00:02:11.080
And so what you really have
to do is think about, well,

00:02:11.080 --> 00:02:13.240
how reliable do I want it to be?

00:02:13.240 --> 00:02:17.350
And the answer should always
be just reliable enough, right?

00:02:17.350 --> 00:02:20.060
That's kind of a jokey
way of responding.

00:02:20.060 --> 00:02:22.638
But think about who
your customers are.

00:02:22.638 --> 00:02:24.430
Like, what do they
really need out of this?

00:02:24.430 --> 00:02:26.597
So you're talking about a
payments processor, right?

00:02:26.597 --> 00:02:28.000
So like, who are your customers?

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:29.620
Is your customers
like your friend

00:02:29.620 --> 00:02:32.350
who sells bracelets at
the farmer's market,

00:02:32.350 --> 00:02:34.960
or is it like a Fortune
500 company who's

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:38.358
going to be like throwing
transactions at you all

00:02:38.358 --> 00:02:39.650
the time from around the world?

00:02:39.650 --> 00:02:41.442
It's going to be very
different situations.

00:02:41.442 --> 00:02:42.550
You know what I mean?

00:02:42.550 --> 00:02:43.258
BRIAN DORSEY: OK.

00:02:43.258 --> 00:02:46.400
So I love this specific thing.

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:49.090
So it's always a
business kind of tradeoff

00:02:49.090 --> 00:02:51.760
or a goals kind of tradeoff,
is the takeaway here.

00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:52.958
STEVE MCGHEE: Totally.

00:02:52.958 --> 00:02:55.000
BRIAN DORSEY: Do you have
any like rules of thumb

00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:57.220
for how do you know--
because a business thing,

00:02:57.220 --> 00:02:59.590
they care about
like money and time.

00:02:59.590 --> 00:03:01.990
Like, how do you
know-- when we're

00:03:01.990 --> 00:03:06.212
moving between these
thresholds, how much is that?

00:03:06.212 --> 00:03:08.170
STEVE MCGHEE: We don't
have a lot of good data.

00:03:08.170 --> 00:03:10.870
It's more just like a sense.

00:03:10.870 --> 00:03:13.030
But the rule of
thumb that we use

00:03:13.030 --> 00:03:16.030
is that every additional
nine you add to a system

00:03:16.030 --> 00:03:19.240
is going to cost you 10 times
as much as the last one did.

00:03:19.240 --> 00:03:21.797
So if you move
from 90% available,

00:03:21.797 --> 00:03:24.130
just like running on your
laptop, and you close the lid,

00:03:24.130 --> 00:03:27.580
and it's not available anymore,
to moving onto the cloud,

00:03:27.580 --> 00:03:28.390
where the VM--

00:03:28.390 --> 00:03:31.090
the single VM that it's
running on is like 99%,

00:03:31.090 --> 00:03:34.750
or actually more
like 99.9% available,

00:03:34.750 --> 00:03:36.970
it's going to-- it
takes more effort.

00:03:36.970 --> 00:03:41.050
And the way the reason about
it is really like 10x per nine.

00:03:41.050 --> 00:03:41.995
So--

00:03:41.995 --> 00:03:43.120
CARTER MORGAN: That's huge.

00:03:43.120 --> 00:03:44.620
STEVE MCGHEE: Yeah,
it's a big deal.

00:03:44.620 --> 00:03:47.110
CARTER MORGAN: I like this
little simple heuristic for me

00:03:47.110 --> 00:03:48.700
to reason about it.

00:03:48.700 --> 00:03:52.450
But I guess I'm still
a little bit lost in--

00:03:52.450 --> 00:03:54.400
like, take the internet.

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:55.900
How reliable is the internet?

00:03:55.900 --> 00:03:58.613
Can we ground it,
maybe these nines,

00:03:58.613 --> 00:04:01.030
in just like a simple like
browsing the web kind of thing?

00:04:01.030 --> 00:04:03.250
How reliable is that?

00:04:03.250 --> 00:04:04.900
STEVE MCGHEE: Yeah,
great question.

00:04:04.900 --> 00:04:07.928
Have you ever turned
off your phone?

00:04:07.928 --> 00:04:09.220
CARTER MORGAN: Rarely, but yes.

00:04:09.220 --> 00:04:11.387
STEVE MCGHEE: Yeah, like
the battery dies sometimes,

00:04:11.387 --> 00:04:13.090
or phones kind of
lose connectivity.

00:04:13.090 --> 00:04:14.560
Like, you ever been
in a dead spot?

00:04:14.560 --> 00:04:16.570
Like, maybe sometimes, right?

00:04:16.570 --> 00:04:19.089
So like, your phone
doesn't have 100%

00:04:19.089 --> 00:04:23.300
reliability in terms of letting
you do stuff on the internet.

00:04:23.300 --> 00:04:25.420
So if you think about
it that way, like,

00:04:25.420 --> 00:04:28.330
if you're building a system
just for yourself on the cloud,

00:04:28.330 --> 00:04:31.510
you wouldn't want to invest in
making it five nines reliable,

00:04:31.510 --> 00:04:34.990
because your pipe to that
service is over your phone,

00:04:34.990 --> 00:04:36.910
and cell towers, and stuff.

00:04:36.910 --> 00:04:39.327
And you're going to be driving
through a tunnel sometimes.

00:04:39.327 --> 00:04:40.750
And so like, why
make a five nine

00:04:40.750 --> 00:04:42.820
service for a
three nines tunnel?

00:04:42.820 --> 00:04:45.490
And before you go too
far with this analogy,

00:04:45.490 --> 00:04:50.133
though, the reason why we start
to have higher availability

00:04:50.133 --> 00:04:51.550
is because a lot
of these services

00:04:51.550 --> 00:04:54.100
are actually for
server-to-server communication

00:04:54.100 --> 00:04:57.100
in a data center, where they
have much better connectivity

00:04:57.100 --> 00:04:58.990
than you do between
your computer

00:04:58.990 --> 00:05:01.870
or your phone and the service.

00:05:01.870 --> 00:05:04.060
And the other part of it
is like, most of the time,

00:05:04.060 --> 00:05:05.393
you have more than one customer.

00:05:05.393 --> 00:05:08.020
So like, if my connectivity
to the service is no good,

00:05:08.020 --> 00:05:09.445
most of the other
people's service

00:05:09.445 --> 00:05:11.890
is actually still good
during that minute

00:05:11.890 --> 00:05:13.240
or during that second, right?

00:05:13.240 --> 00:05:15.640
So you really do
want to have, for

00:05:15.640 --> 00:05:17.020
like these high-end
services, you

00:05:17.020 --> 00:05:19.002
want to have them
pretty highly available.

00:05:19.002 --> 00:05:20.710
But you have to think
of the big picture.

00:05:20.710 --> 00:05:22.000
Like, who's the customer?

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:23.740
How many of the
customers are there?

00:05:23.740 --> 00:05:27.005
How often do they expect
it to be up or not?

00:05:27.005 --> 00:05:28.630
And the last thing
you want to consider

00:05:28.630 --> 00:05:31.690
is like, your service,
or your business,

00:05:31.690 --> 00:05:33.640
or whatever, is probably
made up of a bunch

00:05:33.640 --> 00:05:34.820
of different services.

00:05:34.820 --> 00:05:36.400
And so if you have
many endpoints

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:38.890
that your service might-- or
that your customer may hit,

00:05:38.890 --> 00:05:40.700
some of them might
be super important.

00:05:40.700 --> 00:05:42.680
Some of them might be
way less important.

00:05:42.680 --> 00:05:46.540
So if I need to sign up for
a new account, and it's down,

00:05:46.540 --> 00:05:48.760
I can retry, not a big deal.

00:05:48.760 --> 00:05:52.250
But if I'm sending you thousands
of transactions per second,

00:05:52.250 --> 00:05:54.650
and I want you to like
give me money back,

00:05:54.650 --> 00:05:57.920
that should probably be more
up than the signup flow.

00:05:57.920 --> 00:05:59.127
You know what I mean?

00:05:59.127 --> 00:06:00.710
BRIAN DORSEY: OK,
so this makes sense.

00:06:00.710 --> 00:06:03.800
So we're building this kind
of more reliable system out

00:06:03.800 --> 00:06:06.590
of layers of less reliable
bits, and because we

00:06:06.590 --> 00:06:09.290
have lots of users or
business need to do it.

00:06:09.290 --> 00:06:11.960
But like, so we talked
about a little bit of it.

00:06:11.960 --> 00:06:13.880
Like, in the docs, you
can find out roughly

00:06:13.880 --> 00:06:15.350
how reliable a VM is.

00:06:15.350 --> 00:06:18.680
But when you start to build
these shapes on top of that,

00:06:18.680 --> 00:06:23.480
how do I know even
roughly kind of how

00:06:23.480 --> 00:06:26.687
reliable a particular
pattern that I've built is?

00:06:26.687 --> 00:06:29.020
You know, a load balancer in
front of a managed instance

00:06:29.020 --> 00:06:29.812
group or something.

00:06:29.812 --> 00:06:31.610
STEVE MCGHEE: Yeah,
great question,

00:06:31.610 --> 00:06:34.730
because most
interesting businesses,

00:06:34.730 --> 00:06:37.650
at least that I know of, are not
running on a single VM, right?

00:06:37.650 --> 00:06:40.170
They're not just one thing.

00:06:40.170 --> 00:06:42.410
And if you look at
the cloud offerings,

00:06:42.410 --> 00:06:43.730
there's all kinds of stuff.

00:06:43.730 --> 00:06:45.860
There's like Pub/Sub,
and databases,

00:06:45.860 --> 00:06:47.010
and all kinds of stuff.

00:06:47.010 --> 00:06:51.800
And your job as an
architect, or a designer,

00:06:51.800 --> 00:06:55.070
or an engineer at a company
who's using the cloud

00:06:55.070 --> 00:06:57.762
is to come up with what
we call an architecture.

00:06:57.762 --> 00:06:59.720
So this is the set of
services that all kind of

00:06:59.720 --> 00:07:00.720
stick together.

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:03.890
Some of them are
like you put code

00:07:03.890 --> 00:07:06.350
onto a compute
platform of some kind.

00:07:06.350 --> 00:07:09.470
Another one is like you put
a schema onto a database

00:07:09.470 --> 00:07:11.727
or a data store of some kind.

00:07:11.727 --> 00:07:13.310
Sometimes it's like
a message passing.

00:07:13.310 --> 00:07:15.350
So you have to define
the message cues.

00:07:15.350 --> 00:07:17.040
So this is your architecture.

00:07:17.040 --> 00:07:19.110
So the trick with
architecture is

00:07:19.110 --> 00:07:20.750
you've got a whole
bunch of components.

00:07:20.750 --> 00:07:23.820
And you can compose them
in tons of different ways.

00:07:23.820 --> 00:07:26.720
And some of them are
complete disasters, right?

00:07:26.720 --> 00:07:29.100
Like, some of them
do not work well.

00:07:29.100 --> 00:07:31.820
So now, the question
you ask is like,

00:07:31.820 --> 00:07:33.770
how do we know if we're
building a good one?

00:07:33.770 --> 00:07:36.260
And just like any
software development,

00:07:36.260 --> 00:07:40.400
the best plan is to use
a well-known pattern.

00:07:40.400 --> 00:07:43.520
So we have these design
patterns in computer science.

00:07:43.520 --> 00:07:46.800
We have similar design patterns
in cloud architecture as well.

00:07:46.800 --> 00:07:50.330
So there's actually a paper
that came out sometime

00:07:50.330 --> 00:07:52.340
this year, a while
ago, and it was

00:07:52.340 --> 00:07:56.030
about the-- we called it the
deployment archetypes in Cloud.

00:07:56.030 --> 00:07:57.530
And it gives you a couple--

00:07:57.530 --> 00:08:00.320
like, I think there's six
or eight different models.

00:08:00.320 --> 00:08:04.410
And it starts off simple, like
build one thing in one region.

00:08:04.410 --> 00:08:06.090
And it builds up from there.

00:08:06.090 --> 00:08:07.910
So some of the more
common architectures

00:08:07.910 --> 00:08:10.910
are like, if you have
a stack of services--

00:08:10.910 --> 00:08:13.460
and by services, I
mean like your web

00:08:13.460 --> 00:08:16.340
server, and your database,
and things like that.

00:08:16.340 --> 00:08:18.560
You can have one stack in
one region, and one stack

00:08:18.560 --> 00:08:20.480
in another region, and
like a load balancer

00:08:20.480 --> 00:08:22.340
in front of those two things.

00:08:22.340 --> 00:08:24.470
And you can get really
complicated beyond that.

00:08:24.470 --> 00:08:28.160
You can have things like
failover between those two

00:08:28.160 --> 00:08:29.040
be automated.

00:08:29.040 --> 00:08:30.200
You can have hot and cold.

00:08:30.200 --> 00:08:31.700
You can have hot and warm.

00:08:31.700 --> 00:08:33.080
You can have hot and hot.

00:08:33.080 --> 00:08:35.330
And you can have more
than just two, right?

00:08:35.330 --> 00:08:37.280
You could have n
different deployments

00:08:37.280 --> 00:08:39.360
and have them all be
active at any given time.

00:08:39.360 --> 00:08:40.850
And you can charge
your customers

00:08:40.850 --> 00:08:41.892
across them and all that.

00:08:41.892 --> 00:08:43.789
So take a look at that paper.

00:08:43.789 --> 00:08:47.635
It's not like GCP
architecture specific.

00:08:47.635 --> 00:08:49.010
It gives you the
pattern, though,

00:08:49.010 --> 00:08:51.825
that lets you know what you
can get out of this archetype.

00:08:51.825 --> 00:08:52.950
CARTER MORGAN: I love that.

00:08:52.950 --> 00:08:55.400
And I love just learning
general principles for how

00:08:55.400 --> 00:08:57.170
to architect and design systems.

00:08:57.170 --> 00:08:59.400
So I'm definitely going
to check that out.

00:08:59.400 --> 00:09:02.090
But now this kind of
leads me to this question

00:09:02.090 --> 00:09:04.310
of calling back something
you said earlier,

00:09:04.310 --> 00:09:07.250
getting a little less abstract.

00:09:07.250 --> 00:09:09.710
If I were to take my payment
processor application--

00:09:09.710 --> 00:09:11.990
it was just for myself
and my friends before.

00:09:11.990 --> 00:09:13.880
Now I've got my
first big customer.

00:09:13.880 --> 00:09:15.680
And they're going
to throw thousands

00:09:15.680 --> 00:09:19.740
of receipts and invoices at me.

00:09:19.740 --> 00:09:22.790
How do I start analyzing
going from where

00:09:22.790 --> 00:09:25.100
my offering is at to
where it needs to be now

00:09:25.100 --> 00:09:26.492
to meet these demands?

00:09:26.492 --> 00:09:28.700
STEVE MCGHEE: I love pinning
it to this real problem.

00:09:28.700 --> 00:09:30.770
And the pattern
you're talking about,

00:09:30.770 --> 00:09:32.510
where you start with
a few customers,

00:09:32.510 --> 00:09:35.960
and you slowly grow, and you
start to make like business

00:09:35.960 --> 00:09:37.850
agreements with more
customers-- that

00:09:37.850 --> 00:09:40.070
is common across everybody.

00:09:40.070 --> 00:09:42.560
This is totally how
businesses grow.

00:09:42.560 --> 00:09:44.540
And it's the right way
to do it, because you

00:09:44.540 --> 00:09:46.700
don't want to start
in your garage

00:09:46.700 --> 00:09:48.950
and build like
enterprise software

00:09:48.950 --> 00:09:50.572
and expect it to
be like-- you're

00:09:50.572 --> 00:09:53.030
going to spend 15 years building
this thing before you have

00:09:53.030 --> 00:09:53.863
your first customer.

00:09:53.863 --> 00:09:55.760
So growing with your
customer demands

00:09:55.760 --> 00:09:58.160
is totally the
right way to do it.

00:09:58.160 --> 00:10:00.200
In terms of actually
getting you to be

00:10:00.200 --> 00:10:03.350
able to land that contract
with, you know, BigCo,

00:10:03.350 --> 00:10:05.780
and handle the five
nines or whatever

00:10:05.780 --> 00:10:08.450
it is that they're
putting in your contract,

00:10:08.450 --> 00:10:11.810
this takes a lot of what we
call resilience engineering

00:10:11.810 --> 00:10:13.383
or reliability engineering.

00:10:13.383 --> 00:10:15.050
The way to think about
it is that you're

00:10:15.050 --> 00:10:17.550
going to be building
what we call a platform.

00:10:17.550 --> 00:10:20.130
And this is going to be a
platform of capabilities.

00:10:20.130 --> 00:10:22.820
So these capabilities are
going to be technical things.

00:10:22.820 --> 00:10:27.080
Like, you can deploy code
to your services quickly,

00:10:27.080 --> 00:10:28.430
and effectively, and safely.

00:10:28.430 --> 00:10:30.290
So you've heard of
CI/CD, for example.

00:10:30.290 --> 00:10:33.800
Like, a CI/CD pipeline
is a set of capabilities

00:10:33.800 --> 00:10:35.250
in your platform.

00:10:35.250 --> 00:10:37.250
Other things, like
observability and monitoring,

00:10:37.250 --> 00:10:39.890
so being able to determine,
what is going on right now?

00:10:39.890 --> 00:10:41.690
Why is this one
weird thing slow?

00:10:41.690 --> 00:10:43.390
Like, being able
to look at a graph,

00:10:43.390 --> 00:10:45.140
and being able to drill
into it, and being

00:10:45.140 --> 00:10:48.530
able to check out a
trace or a profile

00:10:48.530 --> 00:10:51.460
of a live running system,
and being able to stand it,

00:10:51.460 --> 00:10:54.877
like having your team be able
to grok what's really going on

00:10:54.877 --> 00:10:56.710
under the covers and
use the tools that they

00:10:56.710 --> 00:10:59.020
have at their disposal-- this
is all part of your platform.

00:10:59.020 --> 00:11:00.520
And you're building
out capabilities

00:11:00.520 --> 00:11:05.290
as a team to be able to run
this super complicated, complex

00:11:05.290 --> 00:11:06.242
system.

00:11:06.242 --> 00:11:07.700
And the more
capabilities you have,

00:11:07.700 --> 00:11:09.525
the better shot you
have at achieving it.

00:11:09.525 --> 00:11:10.900
BRIAN DORSEY: So
if we want to be

00:11:10.900 --> 00:11:14.260
able to handle our requests
from kind of BigCo coming in,

00:11:14.260 --> 00:11:16.150
everything downstream
of that needs

00:11:16.150 --> 00:11:21.730
to be highly reliable to
the same degree, right?

00:11:21.730 --> 00:11:23.590
STEVE MCGHEE: This is a great--

00:11:23.590 --> 00:11:24.760
the answer is no.

00:11:24.760 --> 00:11:27.290
It's one of these
surprising things, where

00:11:27.290 --> 00:11:28.990
what really matters
to your business

00:11:28.990 --> 00:11:30.130
is the front door, right?

00:11:30.130 --> 00:11:32.823
So BigCo has a contract with us.

00:11:32.823 --> 00:11:34.240
And whenever they
send us a thing,

00:11:34.240 --> 00:11:36.850
we have to be able to respond
within some amount of time.

00:11:36.850 --> 00:11:40.450
But does that mean that
the database behind our API

00:11:40.450 --> 00:11:43.270
needs to be like six nines,
and the infrastructure

00:11:43.270 --> 00:11:45.430
that database is on needs
to be like seven nines?

00:11:45.430 --> 00:11:47.530
And then like, does the
power supply behind that--

00:11:47.530 --> 00:11:48.640
CARTER MORGAN: I've never
even heard these nines.

00:11:48.640 --> 00:11:49.600
STEVE MCGHEE: --need
to be like 19 nines?

00:11:49.600 --> 00:11:50.517
Like, what's going on?

00:11:50.517 --> 00:11:52.480
This is untenable, right?

00:11:52.480 --> 00:11:53.950
And the answer is totally no.

00:11:53.950 --> 00:11:56.020
Like, that is not the case.

00:11:56.020 --> 00:11:57.020
So this is good news.

00:11:57.020 --> 00:11:59.680
So you're able to
build things that

00:11:59.680 --> 00:12:02.560
are more reliable on top of
things that are less reliable.

00:12:02.560 --> 00:12:04.330
This is like a
general abstraction

00:12:04.330 --> 00:12:05.510
that you can achieve.

00:12:05.510 --> 00:12:07.930
So if your front door, you
want it to be five nines,

00:12:07.930 --> 00:12:10.750
you can get away with having
like a four nines or even

00:12:10.750 --> 00:12:12.310
a three nines back end.

00:12:12.310 --> 00:12:14.650
And the way you do that
is through some resilience

00:12:14.650 --> 00:12:15.250
capabilities.

00:12:15.250 --> 00:12:16.840
So like, a really,
really simple one

00:12:16.840 --> 00:12:20.510
is purely adding
retries to your system.

00:12:20.510 --> 00:12:24.712
So if your front end is up, and
your back end is down briefly,

00:12:24.712 --> 00:12:26.920
when your front end makes
a request to your back end,

00:12:26.920 --> 00:12:30.700
and it goes, woo, error, instead
of returning the response

00:12:30.700 --> 00:12:33.435
to the customer that you
have an agreement with,

00:12:33.435 --> 00:12:34.810
like, I don't know
what happened.

00:12:34.810 --> 00:12:36.040
Like, that's a bummer.

00:12:36.040 --> 00:12:36.850
Just wait.

00:12:36.850 --> 00:12:41.050
Just wait for 200 milliseconds
or some amount of time

00:12:41.050 --> 00:12:42.260
and try it again.

00:12:42.260 --> 00:12:44.210
If it still doesn't work
wait a little longer.

00:12:44.210 --> 00:12:46.720
So as long as
you're not exceeding

00:12:46.720 --> 00:12:50.200
some sort of contractual
deadline, you can wait all day.

00:12:50.200 --> 00:12:52.480
And you can still give
back a correct response.

00:12:52.480 --> 00:12:55.040
And the customer will
see that as a success.

00:12:55.040 --> 00:12:58.240
So this is another form of
how we kind of hide errors

00:12:58.240 --> 00:13:00.100
behind layers of abstractions.

00:13:00.100 --> 00:13:03.520
And it's basically, you're
trading off-- in this case,

00:13:03.520 --> 00:13:06.070
you're trading off
time for reliability.

00:13:06.070 --> 00:13:09.110
And if that's OK with
your business, do it.

00:13:09.110 --> 00:13:12.520
It's just another
tool in your toolbox.

00:13:12.520 --> 00:13:15.370
CARTER MORGAN: I love this
idea of basically planning

00:13:15.370 --> 00:13:16.180
for the imperfect.

00:13:16.180 --> 00:13:18.488
We talked about this
when we talked before.

00:13:18.488 --> 00:13:20.530
And I'm sure there's a
bunch of different avenues

00:13:20.530 --> 00:13:23.445
that we could go and look
at this, like geography.

00:13:23.445 --> 00:13:24.820
There could be
points of failures

00:13:24.820 --> 00:13:29.290
there, or like you said, with
time, using time as a tradeoff.

00:13:29.290 --> 00:13:31.030
The thing I'm
really curious about

00:13:31.030 --> 00:13:34.630
is, it sounds like there's
a lot that goes into this.

00:13:34.630 --> 00:13:37.030
You talked about a team of
people having to be involved.

00:13:37.030 --> 00:13:38.835
And you yourself are an SRE.

00:13:38.835 --> 00:13:40.960
So it seems like there's
more than just technology.

00:13:40.960 --> 00:13:42.820
And I've been very
focused on that.

00:13:42.820 --> 00:13:44.308
But there's also
people involved.

00:13:44.308 --> 00:13:45.850
And maybe there's
even other elements

00:13:45.850 --> 00:13:47.080
involved in this reliability.

00:13:47.080 --> 00:13:48.770
Can you talk about
that a little bit?

00:13:48.770 --> 00:13:49.750
STEVE MCGHEE: Yeah.

00:13:49.750 --> 00:13:52.270
When we talk about
large systems,

00:13:52.270 --> 00:13:54.710
we tend to say people,
process, and technology.

00:13:54.710 --> 00:13:57.170
So this is a great
example of it.

00:13:57.170 --> 00:14:01.750
So being able to understand
a system, improve the system,

00:14:01.750 --> 00:14:05.290
and build the system is-- it's
being done by humans, right?

00:14:05.290 --> 00:14:09.370
And the model that Google
came up with is called SRE.

00:14:09.370 --> 00:14:11.560
So a lot of these
principles that Google

00:14:11.560 --> 00:14:15.160
used to build these systems out
and to scale them up and not

00:14:15.160 --> 00:14:17.830
burn out all the
humans in the meantime

00:14:17.830 --> 00:14:19.090
has all been written down.

00:14:19.090 --> 00:14:23.440
And the specifics of
what Google SREs would

00:14:23.440 --> 00:14:26.423
do in the past inside of
Google don't apply directly

00:14:26.423 --> 00:14:28.090
to what customers
would do, because it's

00:14:28.090 --> 00:14:29.507
like an entirely
different system.

00:14:29.507 --> 00:14:33.323
And customers aren't
running web search on Borg

00:14:33.323 --> 00:14:34.240
or anything like that.

00:14:34.240 --> 00:14:34.450
CARTER MORGAN: Right.

00:14:34.450 --> 00:14:36.492
STEVE MCGHEE: They're
running payments processors

00:14:36.492 --> 00:14:38.950
on Kubernetes, on Cloud.

00:14:38.950 --> 00:14:41.140
But the principles
are actually the same.

00:14:41.140 --> 00:14:44.080
And the principles can
be applied anywhere.

00:14:44.080 --> 00:14:45.910
They're totally consistent.

00:14:45.910 --> 00:14:49.090
So all of this
bundled together--

00:14:49.090 --> 00:14:53.830
not just the tools, but also
the process and the culture,

00:14:53.830 --> 00:14:56.350
how people work
with each other--

00:14:56.350 --> 00:14:59.800
we kind of bundle
that together as SRE.

00:14:59.800 --> 00:15:02.710
And so this is--
there's a bunch of books

00:15:02.710 --> 00:15:05.440
out there that you can
get that explain all this.

00:15:05.440 --> 00:15:08.930
And it's becoming
more and more popular.

00:15:08.930 --> 00:15:09.430
So--

00:15:09.430 --> 00:15:09.760
BRIAN DORSEY: Awesome.

00:15:09.760 --> 00:15:11.620
So we'll definitely
put some links

00:15:11.620 --> 00:15:14.290
to the SRE books and the
article you mentioned

00:15:14.290 --> 00:15:17.450
earlier in the show notes here.

00:15:17.450 --> 00:15:19.990
So we've got-- as
far as doing this,

00:15:19.990 --> 00:15:23.200
like when you want to actually
kind of push your app up

00:15:23.200 --> 00:15:29.110
to another threshold of nines,
we've got the people, process,

00:15:29.110 --> 00:15:30.610
tech part.

00:15:30.610 --> 00:15:33.370
And then we've got this
reliability patterns.

00:15:33.370 --> 00:15:35.103
And some of those
patterns, I think,

00:15:35.103 --> 00:15:36.520
are built into the
system, like we

00:15:36.520 --> 00:15:39.910
talked about last time, like are
kind of in the VM abstraction.

00:15:39.910 --> 00:15:42.700
And some of them are things
we have to handle with our app

00:15:42.700 --> 00:15:43.860
architecture itself.

00:15:43.860 --> 00:15:45.952
Does that sound fair?

00:15:45.952 --> 00:15:47.413
STEVE MCGHEE: Totally.

00:15:47.413 --> 00:15:49.330
You know, you can--
there's always the idea of

00:15:49.330 --> 00:15:50.760
build versus buy.

00:15:50.760 --> 00:15:53.520
And a lot of these
systems, you're

00:15:53.520 --> 00:15:55.480
just kind of getting
out of the platform.

00:15:55.480 --> 00:15:57.720
So these capabilities
that I talked about,

00:15:57.720 --> 00:16:00.270
if you're running
on-prem on machines

00:16:00.270 --> 00:16:02.010
that you bought a
couple of years ago,

00:16:02.010 --> 00:16:04.238
and you're running your
own software on them,

00:16:04.238 --> 00:16:06.280
you're not going to get
live migration out of it.

00:16:06.280 --> 00:16:07.200
You could build it.

00:16:07.200 --> 00:16:09.930
It might take a while-- or
something like it, right?

00:16:09.930 --> 00:16:13.950
But if you, instead, if you
kind of take that concern,

00:16:13.950 --> 00:16:18.120
and you hand it off to Cloud,
you get this capability.

00:16:18.120 --> 00:16:20.670
So there's a lot
of things out there

00:16:20.670 --> 00:16:23.640
that you can get
a product, right?

00:16:23.640 --> 00:16:26.070
And that gives you that
capability for your platform.

00:16:26.070 --> 00:16:28.110
And now you can do that thing.

00:16:28.110 --> 00:16:31.140
Another similar one is like
if you have the a layer 7 load

00:16:31.140 --> 00:16:33.570
balancer, a global
L7 load balancer,

00:16:33.570 --> 00:16:36.780
you're able to siphon
traffic from one region

00:16:36.780 --> 00:16:38.938
to another, like
kind of magically.

00:16:38.938 --> 00:16:39.480
It's amazing.

00:16:39.480 --> 00:16:41.848
And doing this like on
the Wild West internet

00:16:41.848 --> 00:16:43.390
is really hard,
because it's like you

00:16:43.390 --> 00:16:46.350
got to deal with different
autonomous systems, and BGP,

00:16:46.350 --> 00:16:47.940
and DNS, and all
this crazy stuff.

00:16:47.940 --> 00:16:51.360
But with Google's
L7 load balancer,

00:16:51.360 --> 00:16:53.130
it's pretty straightforward.

00:16:53.130 --> 00:16:56.070
And you take advantage of
this massive set of systems

00:16:56.070 --> 00:16:58.500
out there, this front-end
technology that Google uses

00:16:58.500 --> 00:17:00.420
for its entire set of services.

00:17:00.420 --> 00:17:02.670
CARTER MORGAN: I've got to
thank you, Steve and Brian.

00:17:02.670 --> 00:17:05.670
This is my first time
diving into SRE principles

00:17:05.670 --> 00:17:07.619
and the thinking
behind reliability.

00:17:07.619 --> 00:17:09.329
And so I've learned
so much, whether it's

00:17:09.329 --> 00:17:11.710
about the people, and the
process, and the technology,

00:17:11.710 --> 00:17:15.780
whether it's about having
non-abstract or less abstract

00:17:15.780 --> 00:17:18.579
architectures and really
thinking through it.

00:17:18.579 --> 00:17:19.474
So thank you.

00:17:19.474 --> 00:17:20.849
I'm going to
definitely check out

00:17:20.849 --> 00:17:24.240
that paper about all
the different patterns.

00:17:24.240 --> 00:17:26.069
And for people
listening at home,

00:17:26.069 --> 00:17:27.958
if you check that
paper out, tell us

00:17:27.958 --> 00:17:30.250
in the comments what you
liked or didn't like about it.

00:17:30.250 --> 00:17:31.290
Thank you so much.

00:17:31.290 --> 00:17:34.940
